><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The ankle bounce and A-skipmay be appropriate exerciseswhen runners&#xD;require low plantarflexor loads compared to running.&#xD;• Hurdle jumps produced high soleus loads but low gastrocnemius&#xD;lateralis loads compared to running. This highlights the disparate&#xD;function of the plantarflexors during plyometric exercises.&#xD;• Bounding induced high mechanical loads for both plantarflexors.&#xD;Bounding may be best suited to uninjured runners or when eccentric&#xD;overload is desired.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
